# The tools and charts the owner's mirror copies from their upstreams, and the one file of the platform
# tree that may name such an upstream. scripts/list-mirror-sources.sh writes the list of the mirror from
# it, and the mirror task of the platform tree's publish run copies every row into the registry of
# global.buildRegistry and reads it back there without a login.
#
# One row per line, its fields split by spaces; a number sign starts a comment. A row is one of two kinds:
#   tool <url> <sha256>      a file attached to a GitHub Release, named by the owner, the name, the tag
#                            and the file, each a lower-case path component; <sha256> is that of the file.
#   chart <repository> <chart> <version> <sha256>
#                            a Helm chart from the index of a chart repository served over https, or from
#                            an oci registry, by a lower-case host and path without a trailing slash;
#                            <sha256> is that of the packaged chart: the digest of the version in the
#                            repository index, or the digest of the chart layer of its oci manifest.
# <sha256> is 64 lower-case hex digits. A tool or a chart at one version has one row, whatever reads it.
#
# A BUMP is two releases. First add the row of the new version, with its sha256 from the checksum file of the
# upstream release or, for a chart, from the digest field of its version in the repository index, then land
# and release: the publish run of that tag mirrors the row, and the release gate waits for it. Second, move the
# consumer, by hand or by a sync-versions stamp, and delete the old row once no consumer reads it. A consumer
# moved before its row is red at check time, in scripts/check-mirror-consumers.sh and in the helm dependency
# update of scripts/check.sh. A row no consumer reads yet is listed by that check and fails nothing. The
# version report keeps showing newer upstream versions through the Git tags of the project.
#
# This is the one file of the platform tree that may name an upstream address. A consumer names the owned
# address of its row, scripts/check-mirror-consumers.sh refuses the upstream address in every other file of
# the platform, hostyour-deploy and hostyour-manager trees except the tests that plant it, and holds the
# version and the sha256 of an owned address to its row.
